.0, "sigma_total": 6.72, "baseline_margin": -10.53, "analyst_adjustment": -2.47, "key_factors": ["Strong Republican district lean (R+10 PVI) with incumbent Derek Schmidt running", "Expert ratings consensus of Safe/Solid R across ten raters", "Schmidt's fundraising and cash-on-hand lead over Democrat Don Coover", "No general-election polling available"], "rationale": "The district's R+10 lean, Schmidt's incumbency and primary win, his clear fundraising edge, and a unanimous expert consensus toward Safe/Solid R all point to a comfortable Republican margin, with Polymarket pricing Democratic chances near one in eight. The forecast lands at roughly R+13, modestly beyond the fundamentals-only estimate because the ratings and market reinforce the structural picture.", "watch": "A late surge of Democratic spending or polling showing Coover within single digits would be the first sign the race is closer than the structural numbers suggest.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derek Schmidt (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Kansas' 2nd District is a first-term defense by Republican Derek Schmidt, who won the August primary handily and holds a clear fundraising advantage over Democrat Don Coover, including roughly $940,000 on hand to Coover's $244,000. The district's partisan lean favors Republicans by about ten points, and no general-election polls have been conducted in the race.\n\nEvery major race rater places this in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a one-in-eight chance. Coover has raised a respectable sum for a challenger in this territory, but the combination of incumbency, district lean, and the absence of any independent polling or outside spending on his behalf leaves him a clear underdog heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0506, "margin": -10.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0